      practice, not a vague manager impression.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Train</strong> — a set of learning tools built to replace
      flash cards, not resemble them. It brings a restaurant's menu
      data, back-of-house knowledge, and POS data on what guests
      actually order into a single knowledge base that powers AI-driven
      roleplay a server can practice with on their phone, in minutes —
      with proposed mechanisms, like skill tiers and visible progress a
      server can show a manager, for making that practice something
      staff want to do, not one more mandatory task.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="marketing-section">
  <h2>Why staff would actually want this</h2>
  <p>
    I started in Support, wanting to become a Server — and there was no
    real way to prove I was ready beyond a manager's gut sense in the
    moment. I was already fielding real guest questions at Greet, the
    kind that could have shaped whether a guest decided to come in or
    come back, with no structured way to build or show the skill that
    would have helped me answer them well. A tracked skill profile gives
    someone in that position an actual, legible case to make — not "I
    feel ready," but a real record of what they've already mastered.
  </p>
  <p>
    For servers and bartenders already on the floor, the more honest
    motivator probably isn't the tip on any single check — it's mutual
    respect. Real Assurance and Empathy change the shape of the
    interaction itself: instead of taking an order, the server is
    treated as a knowledgeable professional the guest is glad to have.
    That's a win most servers can feel and want more of on its own
    terms — being sought out, a guest asking for your section, a genuine
    nod of recognition — a quiet form of professional pride that has
    nothing to do with growing any individual check.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this works if the training itself feels like homework. The
    goal is for practicing to feel like sharpening a skill worth being
    proud of — the way a bartender takes pride in a clean pour — not
    compliance training bolted onto the job.
  </p>
</section>

